---
title: A modular Magic engine
blurb: Nine milestones in one night, from an empty Godot project to a Magic rules engine with combat, triggers and layers, built so players can rewrite their own cards.
date: 2026-11-06
tags: [game]
---

I've had an idea kicking around for a while: a roguelike where your deck is made of
Magic: the Gathering cards, and the cards you earn along the way can be changed. Win a
fight and maybe your Lightning Bolt costs nothing now, or your Grizzly Bears pick up
flying, or a spell that hits one creature starts hitting all of them. The game is
called [ModMox](/projects/modmox), and before any of the roguelike part can exist, it
needs something that can actually play Magic. So last night I started on the rules
engine, and by sunrise it was nine milestones in.

The repo's first commit landed a little before 10 p.m. under the name MoxFort, which
lasted about an hour before I renamed it. The ninth milestone finished at quarter to
six in the morning. Here's how the night went, and the two decisions that shaped
everything else.

## The decision that came first

Before writing any engine code, I wrote down eleven design decisions, and the first
one is the reason the rest of this post looks the way it does.

The tempting way to build a card game is to write each card as its own little script.
That works fine until players can modify cards, at which point you're dealing with
cards nobody wrote. A Lightning Bolt with an extra target, or a creature that gained
an ability it was never designed to have, has to behave correctly without me
anticipating it. The only thing I trust to keep those combinations predictable is the
actual structure of Magic's Comprehensive Rules: priority, the stack, state-based
actions, the layer system, replacement effects, all of it.

So the engine follows that structure from day one, even though the card pool is a
handful of Alpha cards. Scope gets cut by leaving out entire mechanics, never by
simplifying the architecture. If no card in the game has a mechanic yet, its rules
don't get written, but the shape of the engine has to leave room for them.

The flip side is that cards are pure data. Every card is a Godot Resource built from
a fixed library of primitives, and every number in an ability is a composable Value,
so "deal 3 damage" can become "deal damage equal to the number of Goblins you control"
with no new code. A card the player owns is stored as an untouched base card plus an
ordered list of Modifications, and the engine flattens it at the start of each fight.
If a card can't be built from the primitives, the answer is a new primitive, never a
one-off script. That's the "modular" part, and it's what the whole game will lean on.

The engine follows current rules and current Oracle wording, even for cards from
1993. That means no mana burn and no damage assignment order. If I ever want one of
those back for flavor, it comes in as a named house rule, kept out of the core.

## Milestone 1: the skeleton

The first milestone was setup. The engine lives in its own Godot addon,
`addons/modmox_rules/`, that never references the game, so it can run headless and
be tested on its own. It got the GUT test framework, a single `tools/test.sh` command
that runs everything from the command line, and enforced static typing, where any
untyped declaration is a compile error.

That last one had a small catch. Godot quietly excludes everything under `addons/`
from its warnings by default, which means my strict typing rules would have skipped
the one folder I most wanted them on. The engine folder is explicitly included again
now, and there's a test that fails if anyone ever undoes that.

## Milestone 2: state and the pregame

Milestone 2 built the bones of a game: players, all seven zones, and game objects.
Following CR 400.7, a card that changes zones becomes a brand new object with a new
ID. It sounds like pedantry, but it's what makes "exile that creature at end of turn"
correctly do nothing if the creature died and came back in the meantime, and it's
notoriously painful to retrofit.

This is also where the event pipeline went in. No rules code ever changes the game
directly. Every change is proposed as an event, offered to replacement effects,
applied, and logged, even though there were no replacement effects or triggers yet.
Retrofitting that would have meant rewriting every rule that touches the state.

The pregame came along with it: a coin flip, the play or draw choice, London
mulligans with ordered bottoming, and Free Mulligans, which a character in the
roguelike might earn. I also tracked down a memory leak caused by a reference cycle
between game objects and their zones. The suite went from 4 tests to 51.

## The decision that makes saves easy

Milestone 2 is also where the second big decision first showed up in code. The engine never waits for
input. Whenever a player has a choice, it stops and hands back a Pending Decision
listing the legal options, then picks back up when it gets an answer. The GUI and the
AI answer exactly the same decisions, and neither one ever reaches into the engine.

Combine that with determinism and you get some nice things for free. Every encounter
is fully reproducible from its setup, including a random seed, plus the answers given.
There are two separate random streams, one for the game and one for the AI, so tuning
the AI never changes a shuffle, and an AI looking ahead can't predict the real deck
order.

That means a mid-fight save is just the setup and the list of answers, and loading
it replays them. I considered saving the whole game state, but every new class would
have had to stay in sync with the save format forever. The tradeoff is that an engine
update can invalidate a save from the middle of a fight, and for a roguelike,
restarting that one fight is a fair price. It also makes reloading useless for
rerolling bad draws, since the shuffle comes out the same every time.

## Milestone 3: turns and priority

Milestone 3 added every phase and step of a turn and priority passing, with no cards
at all yet. The starting player skips their first draw, the blockers and damage steps
get skipped when nobody attacks, and cleanup makes you discard down to your maximum
hand size.

The interesting choice here was how to answer a priority decision. Each one lists
every legal Action in a fixed order, and the answer is just an index into that list.
I considered answering with the Action's data, like "cast object 15," but that needs a
second set of validation rules that must always agree with the legality checks.
With an index, validation is a bounds check, and an illegal play can't even be
written down. At this point the only Action that existed was passing, which made for
some very polite games.

## Milestone 4: lands and mana

Milestone 4 brought the five basic lands, playing a land, tapping for mana, and the
mana pool emptying between steps. Basic lands don't actually have a mana ability
written on them. The engine grants it from the land's subtype, the way CR 305.6 does,
so an effect that turns a Forest into an Island will change what it taps for with no
special handling.

The mana pool holds individual units of mana instead of a count per color. Each unit
records its type and where it came from, because later cards care about things like
"spend this only on creature spells" or "whenever you tap a land for mana," and a
count can't carry that.

## Milestone 5: casting and the stack

Milestone 5 was the biggest so far. It added costs, targets, Filters, Values, spells
resolving off the stack, and spells fizzling when their targets disappear. The first
real spells were Lightning Bolt, Ancestral Recall and Dark Ritual. Colors now come
from the mana cost instead of being set by hand, and the same characteristics work
outside a fight, so the deck screen and the battlefield can never disagree about a
card.

It also added undo. You can take back recent choices, like tapping the wrong land or
starting a spell you didn't mean to cast, up to the last point where taking it back
wouldn't be fair, such as after a card was drawn or the other player got a decision.
The engine keeps a checkpoint copy of the state at each of those points, and undoing
restores the checkpoint and replays the few decisions since. Replaying the whole fight
from the start would have worked, but people undo several times in a row when
untapping lands. Writing an exact reverse for every kind of event was the other
option, and that's the kind of hand-written logic that quietly drifts out of date.

## Milestone 6: creatures and combat

Milestone 6 brought creatures: Grizzly Bears, Hill Giant and Elvish Archers. That
meant power and toughness, summoning sickness, attacking, blocking, combat damage,
and the keywords first strike, double strike, trample and flash. Following current
rules, an attacker blocked by several creatures divides its damage however it likes
instead of in a set order.

Declaring attackers didn't fit the index-into-a-list idea, since you'd need an
option for every possible group of attackers. Those decisions are answered with plain
lists instead, like the IDs of the attacking creatures or one damage amount per
blocker, and each entry is still checked against the candidates.

This was also the milestone where I started deliberately breaking the rules. After
everything passes, I change a rule in the engine on purpose and make sure some test
fails. The 225 tests caught all 18 of those mutations. The funny part is that
creatures could fight but not die yet, since that's a state-based action, and those
were next.

## Milestone 7: state-based actions

Milestone 7 made things lethal. The engine now checks state-based actions every time
a player would receive priority, performs everything that applies at once, and
repeats until nothing does. Players lose at 0 life or after drawing from an empty
library, and creatures die from lethal damage or 0 toughness. Fights can end in a
win or a draw, and you can concede at any time, which goes into the decision log so
it replays too.

The milestone 6 tests needed updating, because creatures that had been surviving
lethal damage were now, correctly, dying. Thirteen of sixteen rule mutations were
caught, and the other three turned out to behave identically under the current rules.

## Milestone 8: triggered abilities

Milestone 8 added triggered abilities, with Ankh of Mishra, Howling Mine and Ivory
Tower as the first artifacts. Triggers fire at the beginning of a step, when
something changes zones, or when a spell is cast, and "intervening if" clauses get
checked both when the ability triggers and when it resolves.

The tricky part was timing. I first considered scanning the event log only when
someone would receive priority, but by then the board has moved on, and a creature
that left the battlefield in between would miss its own trigger. Instead, triggers
are detected right after each event, or each batch of simultaneous events, like all
the combat damage in a step. Leaves-the-battlefield triggers look back in time, so a
creature that dies alongside others sees all of them die, including itself. Every
other trigger looks at the game after the batch. That needed last-known information,
so a creature's characteristics get frozen as it leaves. This milestone also added
Emblems, which is how the roguelike's boss rules and character Statuses will reach
the engine without it ever knowing those exist.

## Milestone 9: static abilities and layers

The last milestone of the night was the layer system, the part of Magic's rules that
decides what a creature actually is once a bunch of effects pile up. The first cards
to use it were Giant Growth, Crusade, Serra Angel, Giant Spider, Wall of Stone and
Keldon Warlord, along with flying, reach, vigilance, haste, deathtouch and defender.

The engine works out every object on the battlefield at once, one layer at a time,
because that's how the rules actually work. A filter like "creatures with flying get
+1/+1" has to see the result of earlier layers, and computing one object at a time
would either get that wrong or recurse forever. The result is cached until the next
event, and since every change is an event, throwing the cache away there is always
correct. Timestamps turned out to be free, too. Every object entering the battlefield
gets the next ID, so an object's ID already is its timestamp. The 321 tests caught all
24 rule mutations.

## Where it stands

By morning the engine could play a real, if small, game of Alpha-era Magic, with
eighteen decision records explaining why it works the way it does. Next up is
replacement and prevention effects, and after that the first playable slice of Alpha
against a simple AI. There's no GUI yet, and none of the roguelike exists. But the
part I was most worried about, whether a modified card would still behave like a real
Magic card, finally feels like it has solid ground under it.
